# .github/ is not ignored. It held four workflows - check, publish-docker,
# release-desktop and the screenshot pipeline - and none of them were tracked, so
# the project had no CI on its default branch at all: no checks on a push, no Docker
# publishing, no desktop releases. A workflow file that is not in the repository is
# a file that never runs for anyone else.
#
# publish-docker.yml is the exception and stays untracked until someone decides.
# It builds and pushes an image on *every* push to main, so tracking it would start
# publishing images for every commit from now on rather than resuming something that
# had been switched off deliberately. That is a decision about their infrastructure,
# not a cleanup.

# See above. Builds and pushes an image on every push to main, so it stays out
# until that is a decision someone makes on purpose rather than by accident.
/.github/workflows/publish-docker.yml

# shots.yml runs the screenshot suite, which is scaffolding for UI work rather than
# something every push should pay for. It lives on a shots/* branch together with the
# scripts it drives - tests/browser/, ignored above as /tests/ - so on main it would
# reference a directory that does not exist, which is the same mistake the browser
# job in check.yml was making.
/.github/workflows/shots.yml
